  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{27}$ as a power of $3$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}={3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{1}{27})={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=3$ and $x=-3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{-3})=-3\n$$",
